PM / devfreq: do not start the monitor on a suspended device

devfreq_suspend_device() stops the governor's monitor, but a later
governor change reaches devfreq_monitor_start() through DEVFREQ_GOV_START
and starts it again with no reference to the suspended state -
suspend_count is consulted nowhere outside devfreq_suspend_device() and
devfreq_resume_device(). Writing to the governor sysfs attribute while a
device is runtime suspended therefore resumes polling behind the driver's
back.

For most drivers that is wasted work. For one whose device is unreachable
while suspended it is not: devfreq_monitor() calls the profile's target()
on every tick without comparing against previous_freq, so the samples it
feeds in are idle readings taken from powered-down hardware, and they
overwrite the frequency policy that was in force before the suspend. The
device then comes back at the wrong rate.

Leave the monitor stopped in that case. The delayed work is still
initialised first, because devfreq_monitor_resume() queues that work when
the device comes back and would otherwise queue something uninitialised.

Verified on an ArmSoM Sige5 by selecting simple_ondemand for the first
time while the GPU was already runtime suspended: no polling starts, the
log stays clean where it previously took 5348 "dvfs failed with (-11)"
messages at the 50 ms poll interval, and polling begins normally on the
next resume.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c	2026-07-24 14:17:26.000000000 +0000
+++ b/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c	2026-08-02 21:50:21.123809052 +0000
@@ -499,6 +499,20 @@
 		goto out;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A governor selected while the device is suspended must not start
+	 * polling it: the driver has already told us the hardware is not
+	 * there, and sampling it produces idle readings that overwrite
+	 * whatever policy was in force before the suspend. The work is
+	 * initialised above regardless, so devfreq_monitor_resume() has
+	 * something valid to queue once the device comes back.
+	 */
+	if (atomic_read(&devfreq->suspend_count)) {
+		devfreq->stop_polling = true;
+		mutex_unlock(&devfreq->lock);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (devfreq->profile->polling_ms)
 		queue_delayed_work(devfreq_wq, &devfreq->work,
 			msecs_to_jiffies(devfreq->profile->polling_ms));
